# CSS :has() Selector — Selecting Elements Based on Their Children

## 1. What Is the `:has()` Selector?

Traditionally, CSS selectors allow us to select elements based on their own properties, classes, IDs, and relationships with other elements.

But what if you want to style a parent element depending on what child it contains?

That's where the `:has()` pseudo-class comes in.

The `:has()` selector allows CSS to select an element if it contains another element matching a specified condition.

It is often called the **CSS parent selector**.

## 2. Why Is It Useful?

Before `:has()`, developers often needed JavaScript to detect certain child elements and apply styles to their parents.

Now, many of these styling tasks can be handled directly using CSS.

Common use cases:

- Highlighting a form when an input is invalid.
- Styling cards that contain images.
- Changing layouts based on child elements.
- Highlighting navigation items with specific content.
- Applying styles to containers based on their contents.

## 3. Basic Syntax

```css
parent:has(child) {
    /* CSS styles */
}
```

The parent is selected only when it contains a matching descendant.

For example:

```html
<div class="card">
    <h2>Product</h2>
    <img src="product.jpg" alt="Product">
</div>
```

```css
.card:has(img) {
    border: 2px solid green;
}
```

The card receives a green border because it contains an image.

If the image is removed, the selector no longer matches.

## 4. A Practical Example: Highlighting Forms

Imagine a form where you want to highlight the entire input group when its input is invalid.

```html
<div class="form-group">
    <label for="email">Email</label>
    <input
        type="email"
        id="email"
        required
    >
</div>
```

```css
.form-group:has(input:invalid) {
    border: 2px solid red;
    padding: 10px;
}
```

The `.form-group` receives a red border when its input matches the `:invalid` pseudo-class.

This allows CSS to react to the state of a child element without JavaScript.

## 5. Using `:has()` With Multiple Conditions

You can use multiple selectors inside `:has()`.

```css
.card:has(img, video) {
    background-color: lightgray;
}
```

This selects a card if it contains either an image or a video.

You can also combine conditions:

```css
.card:has(img):has(button) {
    border: 2px solid blue;
}
```

This selects cards containing both an image and a button.

## 6. Using `:has()` With Direct Children

Sometimes you only want to check direct children rather than any nested descendant.

```css
.container:has(> img) {
    padding: 20px;
}
```

The `>` combinator means the image must be a direct child of `.container`.

Compare:

```css
.container:has(img) {
    /* Matches a descendant image */
}
```

```css
.container:has(> img) {
    /* Matches only a direct child image */
}
```

This distinction is important when working with deeply nested HTML structures.

## 7. Using `:has()` for Navigation Styling

Imagine a navigation menu where an item should receive a special style if it contains a dropdown.

```html
<nav>
    <ul>
        <li class="menu-item">
            <a href="#">Home</a>
        </li>

        <li class="menu-item">
            <a href="#">Services</a>
            <ul>
                <li><a href="#">Design</a></li>
                <li><a href="#">Development</a></li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
</nav>
```

```css
.menu-item:has(ul) {
    font-weight: bold;
    color: blue;
}
```

Only menu items containing a nested list receive the special styling.

## 8. `:has()` vs Traditional CSS Selectors

| Selector | What it does |
|---|---|
| `div p` | Selects paragraphs inside div elements |
| `div > p` | Selects direct child paragraphs |
| `div + p` | Selects the paragraph immediately following a div |
| `div ~ p` | Selects paragraphs following a div with the same parent |
| `div:has(p)` | Selects div elements containing a paragraph |

The key difference is that `:has()` allows the selection condition to depend on matching related elements.

## 9. Important Things to Know

- `:has()` is a relational pseudo-class.
- It can select a parent based on its descendants or related elements.
- It supports multiple selectors.
- It can be combined with other pseudo-classes.
- It is supported in modern browsers, but check compatibility if your project needs older browsers.
- Complex selectors can have performance implications, so use them thoughtfully.

## 10. Common Mistakes

- Confusing `:has()` with `:is()` or `:where()`.
- Forgetting that `:has()` selects the element before the parentheses.
- Using `>` when you actually need to match nested descendants.
- Assuming `:has()` automatically changes HTML or adds functionality. It only applies CSS matching and styling.
- Ignoring browser compatibility requirements.

## 11. Key Takeaways

- `:has()` allows CSS to select elements based on matching related elements.
- It is commonly used to style parents based on their children.
- It can reduce the need for JavaScript in certain styling scenarios.
- It supports multiple conditions and combinators.
- It makes CSS more expressive and powerful.

## One-Line Summary

**CSS `:has()` allows developers to select and style elements based on the elements they contain or their relationships with other elements.**
